[[ ## response ## ]]
<<on the use of the silent operator>>

While the `@` operator is often used in the code body to silence warnings, it is rarely strictly necessary in modern PHP development. Most developers prefer explicit checks to handle potential failures before they trigger an error inside the function body.

* In the body of logic where you interact with legacy systems, `@` might be used as a temporary patch.
* If you are processing a large request body, silencing a stream warning can sometimes mask underlying network issues.
* Modern error handling typically catches exceptions rather than relying on the silent operator in the main body of your script.
* You should always attempt to write a clean function body that validates inputs instead of suppressing output.
* The body of the application remains more maintainable when errors are logged and handled rather than hidden.
* Ultimately, the body of PHP best practices suggests avoiding `@` in favor of defensive coding patterns.

Postscript: Always prefer explicit error handling over suppression.

